          "heading": "The Print Revolution and Its Impact",
          "para": "The shift from hand printing to mechanical printing is called the Print Revolution, and it changed far more than technology. Before Gutenberg, a scribe might copy one book in months; a press with several workers could print hundreds of sheets a day, so the time and labour of producing books fell dramatically and multiple identical copies became available. Books could now reach wider circles of readers, and printed knowledge could be preserved in standard form instead of changing with every copyist. Historians emphasise that the print revolution led to a new 'reading public': not one uniform public, but several overlapping publics - the urban elite reading expensive editions, students and artisans reading cheap pamphlets, and, later, whole new groups of readers such as women and workers. Print also changed how people thought: ideas could now be compared across books, debated in public, and carried across borders faster than any traveller. In this sense print did not only record the modern world; it actively created it by standardising languages, spreading information, and forming communities of readers who had never met.",
